ving a pattern across path graphs shows that the Roman weight is at least R+1 where R is the graph radius. Attempting a proof by contradiction, hypothesizing that a Roman dominating function of weight less than or equal to R exists. Taking any shortest path P from a center `c` to a vertex `x` with distance R, where the path has R+1 vertices.\n\nAnalyzing each vertex on the path P must be Roman dominated with weight 0, 1, or 2. Determining if a weight-2 vertex can dominate vertices on P separated by a distance of 3 or more is key. Examining the path P, a vertex of weight 2 cannot dominate multiple vertices with separation greater than or equal to 3.\n\n\n**Bounding Dominating Sets Using Path**\n\nA vertex with weight 2 dominates at most 3 vertices on a shortest path due to distance limitations. A vertex with weight 1 dominates at most 1 vertex on the same path. The path from the center to a farthest vertex contains R+1 vertices.\n\nConstructing relationships between the Roman weight W, set sizes |S_1|, |S_2|, and radius R is crucial. The path's length, R+1, is bounded by 3|S_2| + |S_1|. The Roman weight W is defined as 2|S_2| + |S_1|.\n\nIf there's only one branch of depth R from the center, the center can shift. This implies at least two such branches and a diameter of at least 2R-1. A diametral path includes 2R vertices, which can be used to improve the bound.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_full.lean\"","EndLine":"440","StartLine":"420","toolAction":"\"Viewing test_full.lean\"","toolSummary":"\"View lines 420-440 of test_full.lean\""}}]}
