and k is found.\n\nWhen k=1, the condition,  d* >= |V0| - k + 1, always holds true, which means the proof strategy works. However, when k >= 2, the analysis demonstrates that large |V0| values, relative to k, create specific graph structures, which are either dominated by stars or have dense clusters around V2, meaning their radius is small. The maximum radius is at most 3.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\n\\n# Check if there is ANY graph with:\\n# two vertices u1, u2 with f(u1)=2, f(u2)=2\\n# and |V_0| >= 4 (meaning at least 4 vertices dominated by u1 or u2)\\n# and radius(G) >= 4?\\nfound = False\\nfor n in range(6, 20):\\n    for p in [0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3]:\\n        for _ in range(50):\\n            G = nx.erdos_renyi_graph(n, p)\\n            if nx.is_connected(G):\\n                # find if there exist u1, u2\\n                for u1 in G.nodes():\\n                    for u2 in G.nodes():\\n                        if u2 <= u1: continue\\n                        # V0 vertices adjacent to u1 or u2:\\n                        n12 = (set(G.neighbors(u1)) | set(G.neighbors(u2))) - {u1, u2}\\n                        if len(n12) >= 4:\\n                            # if all vertices in G are in n12 | {u1, u2} | V1:\\n                            # What if V1 is empty? (all vertices in n12 | {u1, u2})\\n                            if len(n12) + 2 == n: # V1 is empty\\n                                rad = nx.radius(G)\\n                                if rad >= 4:\\n                                    print('FOUND! rad =', rad)\\n                                    found = True\\n                                    break\\n                    if found: break\\n            if found: break\\n        if found: break\\n    if found: break\\n\\nif not found:\\n    print('When V1 is empty and k=2: radius(G) >= 4 NEVER HAPPENS!')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd"